//? Title: Iris linear discriminant analysis
//? Subtitle: Original four-feature fit, actual posterior predictions and a separate held-out petal model
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Linear discriminant analysis compares classes through a shared within-class
covariance. We derive its affine Bayes scores with a tiny checkable fit, retain
the original four-measurement Iris analysis and new-flower predictions, and add
a separate two-petal model whose genuine two-dimensional decision regions are
scored on held-out rows. Training accuracy, discriminant trace proportions and
posterior probabilities answer different questions.
)

\toc

> Shared covariance gives affine scores

Assume class k has Gaussian predictor distribution with mean mu_k, common
positive-definite covariance Sigma and prior pi_k. Dropping the common quadratic
term from log(pi_k*f_k(x)) leaves

$$\delta_k(x)=x^T\Sigma^{-1}\mu_k-\tfrac12\mu_k^T\Sigma^{-1}\mu_k+\log\pi_k,\quad P(k\mid x)=\frac{\exp(\delta_k-\delta_{max})}{\sum_j\exp(\delta_j-\delta_{max})}.$$
\alttext(Affine class scores determine linear decision boundaries; a maximum-shifted softmax converts them into model posteriors.)

The fitted pooled covariance is W/(n−K), where W sums each row’s deviations from
its own class mean. This is the unbiased within-class estimate, not W/n. The
native model solves with a Cholesky factor and caches precision-weighted means.
A common covariance gives affine score differences, so pairwise boundaries are
lines in two predictors. Posterior confidence is conditional on the model
assumptions and priors; it is not a calibration certificate.

> Tiny symmetric fit

Use one predictor and class A values−3,−2,−1, class B values1,2,3. Actual means
are−2 and2; pooled variance is1 and equal priors give delta_B−delta_A=4*x. At x0
the posterior tie is broken by the alphabetical first class. Every displayed
probability below comes from the native fitted model.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lda_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/lda.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;
    d.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{-3, -2, -1, 1, 2, 3});
    d.add_column("class", std::vector<std::string>{"A", "A", "A", "B", "B", "B"});
    dm::stats::LDA m(d, "class ~ x");
    dm::dstruct::DataFrame q;
    q.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{-1, 0, 1});
    auto p = m.predict_detail(q);
    std::print("{}\n| actual query x | actual selected class | actual P(A) | actual P(B) |\n| ---: | --- | " "---: | ---: |\n",
               m.group_means_frame().to_md());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} |\n", int(i) - 1,
                   p.class_label[i], p.posterior[i][0], p.posterior[i][1]);
    dm::graphics::Diagram g;
    g.node("means", {0, 2},
           std::format("Actual fitted means\nA{}; B{}", m.group_means()[0][0],
                       m.group_means()[1][0])).node("prior", {0, -2},
                                                    std::format(
                                                    "Actual fitted priors\n{}, {}",
                                                    m.priors()[0], m.priors()[1])).node("tie", {7, 0}, std::format("Actual x0 prediction\n{}; P(B){}", p.class_label[1], p.posterior[1][1])).edge("means", "tie").edge("prior", "tie").save_svg("build/lda_tiny.svg");
}
```
