> A separate two-petal classifier and held-out score

Use the existing native stratified split with test fraction0.2 and seed42,
giving120 training and30 test rows with10 test rows per species. Fit only
Petal.Length and Petal.Width on the training partition. The two-dimensional
regions below are therefore the complete two-feature model’s true
predictor-space classifier, rather than a slice through a four-feature model.
Only training rows appear as points in the decision figure. The test partition
is used once for the printed confusion counts and accuracy; no hyperparameter
selection is performed. One small seeded split is descriptive evidence, not a
precise estimate of generalization or a universal ranking of feature choices.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lda_petals.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/datasets/datasets.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/lda.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/train_test_split.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/metrics.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto[train, test] =
        dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::iris(), .2, 42, "Species");
    dm::stats::LDA m(train, "Species ~ Petal.Length + Petal.Width");
    auto p = m.predict(test);
    dm::nn::ConfusionMatrix cm(3);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < test.nrows(); ++i) {
        auto truth = test.string_column("Species").at(i);
        auto a =
            std::find(m.classes().begin(), m.classes().end(), truth) -
            m.classes().begin(), b =
            std::find(m.classes().begin(), m.classes().end(), p[i]) -
            m.classes().begin();
        cm.add(a, b);
    }
    std::print("| actual training rows | actual test rows | actual training accuracy | actual test accuracy " "|\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| {} | {} | {:.10f} | {:.10f} |\n\n| actual test truth | " "predicted setosa | predicted versicolor | predicted virginica |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: " "|\n",
               train.nrows(), test.nrows(), m.training_accuracy(), cm.accuracy());
    for (std::size_t c = 0; c < 3; ++c)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", m.classes()[c], cm.cells()[c][0],
                   cm.cells()[c][1], cm.cells()[c][2]);
    m.plot_decision_regions().size(950, 700).y_limits(-.2, 3.5).title(
        "Training-only two-petal LDA decision regions").save_svg(
        "build/lda_petals.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual training rows | actual test rows | actual training accuracy | actual test accuracy |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 120 | 30 | 0.9666666667 | 0.9333333333 |

| actual test truth | predicted setosa | predicted versicolor | predicted virginica |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| setosa | 10 | 0 | 0 |
| versicolor | 0 | 9 | 1 |
| virginica | 0 | 1 | 9 |
\image(figures/lda_petals.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Complete two-petal decision regions fitted only to the training partition.)
\alttext(Every overlaid point is a training observation; separate printed test counts measure held-out predictions without leaking test rows into fitting.)

> Contracts and verification

Fitting owns the resolved formula design, class means, precision terms, axes and
retained training data. Null response/predictor rows are excluded; inference
restores missing-predictor rows as empty labels and empty posterior/score rows.
The numerical design must be finite and the pooled scatter positive definite. At
least two observed classes, two rows per class and more usable rows than classes
plus design columns are required. No shrinkage fallback handles singular
covariance. User priors must be positive, match alphabetical class order and sum
to1 within1e-6. Predictor-space regions require exactly two resolved predictor
columns.

Independent NumPy pooled covariance, Gaussian Bayes scores, posterior
normalization, axis normalization and centered projections verified both the
original full-data four-feature model and the separate held-out two-petal model.
Both complete-data two/four-feature models also matched frozen means,
predictions, posteriors and discriminants exactly. Native tests add an analytic
one-predictor posterior/tie control, independently reconstructed two-by-two
covariance on all30 held-out rows, priors/predictor-only inference checks and
the existing original Iris regression suite. Core numerical behavior is
unchanged. The complete ten-report batch passed77 native CTest cases,39 directly
instrumented ASan/UBSan cases,106 compiled/executed public examples,479/479
scoped definitions and ten representative clangd hovers. All5 complete report
programs executed and all5 native figures were visually reviewed. HTML/Markdown
computed result and identical source/build/export figure bytes passed
verification. Repeatable receipts are retained under
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-194-203/`.

> References

R. A. Fisher(1936),
[The Use of Multiple Measurements in Taxonomic Problems|https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1809.1936.tb02137.x],
Annals of Eugenics7(2),179–188. The fitted implementation combines
pooled-covariance discriminant axes with Gaussian Bayes class posteriors; the
distributional and calibration assumptions are explicit above. Dataset values
and source provenance come from the native embedded Iris loader.
